  <!-- ============ WHY WARP ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="why-warp">
    <span class="section-label">Overview</span>
    <h2>Why Warp for VibeCrew</h2>
    <p>VibeCrew works with any terminal. But Warp unlocks capabilities that directly improve multi-session AI development: structured output blocks for reviewing agent work, launch configurations for instant workspace setup, Warp Drive for sharing workflows across your team, and session restoration so you never lose your layout.</p>

    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Blocks</h4>
        <p>Every command is a discrete, navigable, copyable block. When Claude Code produces 200 lines of output, you can scroll within that block without losing your place in the session.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Launch Configurations</h4>
        <p>Save your multi-project workspace as a YAML file. One click and every project opens with Planning, Development, and Terminal panes ready to go.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Warp Drive</h4>
        <p>Share VibeCrew workflows, environment variables, and runbook notebooks across your team. Everyone gets the same tooling, instantly synced.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Session Restoration</h4>
        <p>Quit Warp, restart your Mac, open Warp &mdash; your tabs, panes, and recent blocks are restored exactly as you left them.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>This page is the single reference for everything Warp + VibeCrew &mdash; from installation through advanced features.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ INSTALL ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="install">
    <span class="section-label">Getting Started</span>
    <h2>Quick Install</h2>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <h4>Download Warp</h4>
        <p>Install from <strong>warp.dev</strong>. Available on macOS, Linux, and Windows.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>Install the Claude Code notification plugin</h4>
        <p>Warp has an official Claude Code integration plugin that provides native notifications when a session completes or needs permission. Run these commands <strong>inside a Claude Code session</strong> (not directly in Warp):</p>
        <pre class="copyable" data-copy="/plugin marketplace add warpdotdev/claude-code-warp"><button class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy to clipboard"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="9" y="9" width="13" height="13" rx="2"/><path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"/></svg></button><code><span class="code-label">claude code</span>/plugin marketplace add warpdotdev/claude-code-warp</code></pre>
        <pre class="copyable" data-copy="/plugin install warp@claude-code-warp"><button class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy to clipboard"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="9" y="9" width="13" height="13" rx="2"/><path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"/></svg></button><code><span class="code-label">claude code</span>/plugin install warp@claude-code-warp</code></pre>
        <p>After running both commands, <strong>restart Claude Code</strong> for notifications to activate.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>Install terminal-notifier</h4>
        <p>Required for VibeCrew's <code>notify.sh</code> hook script to send macOS banner notifications.</p>
        <pre class="copyable" data-copy="brew install terminal-notifier"><button class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy to clipboard"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="9" y="9" width="13" height="13" rx="2"/><path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"/></svg></button><code><span class="code-label">bash</span>brew install terminal-notifier</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>Send a test notification</h4>
        <p><code>terminal-notifier</code> won't appear in System Settings until it has sent at least one notification. Send a test to register it:</p>
        <pre class="copyable" data-copy="terminal-notifier -message &quot;VibeCrew test&quot; -title &quot;Test Notification&quot;"><button class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy to clipboard"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="9" y="9" width="13" height="13" rx="2"/><path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"/></svg></button><code><span class="code-label">bash</span>terminal-notifier -message "VibeCrew test" -title "Test Notification"</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>Enable notifications</h4>
        <p>Now open <strong>System Settings &rarr; Notifications &rarr; terminal-notifier</strong> (it appears after the first notification) and set alert style to <strong>Banners</strong> or <strong>Alerts</strong>. Also enable notifications in <strong>Warp Settings &rarr; Features &rarr; Desktop Notifications</strong>.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ DEEP-LINKING ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="deep-linking">
    <span class="section-label">Notifications</span>
    <h2>Deep-Linking &amp; Notifications</h2>
    <p>The killer feature of Warp for VibeCrew: when a notification fires, clicking it takes you to the <em>exact tab</em> that needs attention &mdash; no hunting through multiple sessions.</p>

    <h3>Terminal comparison</h3>
    <TableWrap>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Terminal</th><th>Notification Method</th><th>Tab Deep-Link</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Warp</strong></td><td>OSC 777 + macOS banner</td><td>Yes &mdash; click goes to exact tab</td></tr>
          <tr><td>iTerm2</td><td>OSC 9 + macOS banner</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>VS Code Terminal</td><td>macOS banner</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Terminal.app</td><td>macOS banner</td><td>No</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </TableWrap>

    <h3>How it works</h3>
    <p>Warp exposes a <code>WARP_SESSION_ID</code> environment variable for each terminal tab. VibeCrew captures this value and constructs a <code>warp://session/&lt;id&gt;</code> URI. When the <code>notify.sh</code> script fires a notification, it passes this URI to <code>terminal-notifier</code>'s <code>-execute</code> flag. Clicking the macOS banner executes the deep link, which brings Warp to the foreground and switches to the exact tab.</p>

    <div class="flow"><span class="muted">Agent blocks on permission</span>
    &darr;
<span class="highlight">notify.sh</span> reads JSON from stdin, extracts notification_type
    &darr;
Captures <span class="highlight">$WARP_SESSION_ID</span> from environment
    &darr;
Constructs deep link: <span class="highlight">warp://session/$SESSION_ID</span>
    &darr;
Dispatches via <span class="highlight">terminal-notifier</span> -execute "open '$DEEP_LINK'"
    &darr;
<span class="muted">You click the notification &rarr; Warp focuses the exact tab</span></div>

    <h3>Verify your session ID</h3>
    <p>In any Warp tab, run:</p>
    <pre class="copyable" data-copy="echo $WARP_SESSION_ID"><button class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy to clipboard"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="9" y="9" width="13" height="13" rx="2"/><path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"/></svg></button><code><span class="code-label">bash</span>echo $WARP_SESSION_ID</code></pre>
    <p>This should return a UUID. If it's empty, update Warp to the latest version.</p>

    <Callout type="info" title="Using a different terminal?">
      <p>VibeCrew works with any terminal. You'll get macOS banner notifications regardless. The only difference is that non-Warp terminals can't deep-link to a specific tab &mdash; clicking the notification brings the terminal app to focus but you'll need to find the tab manually.</p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ LAUNCH CONFIG ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="launch-config">
    <span class="section-label">Window Layout</span>
    <h2>The Ideal Window Layout</h2>
    <p>Warp Launch Configurations let you save a complete terminal layout &mdash; windows, tabs, panes, working directories, and startup commands &mdash; as a YAML file. Open it with one click and your entire VibeCrew workspace is ready.</p>

    <h3>One launch config per project, three panes each</h3>
    <p>VibeCrew creates <strong>one launch config per project</strong>. Each config opens a single tab with three split panes so you can plan, build, and run servers without switching context. Launch only the projects you need &mdash; no wasted resources.</p>

    <div class="layout-diagram">
      <div class="layout-pane layout-planning">
        <div class="layout-pane-title">Planning</div>
        <div class="layout-pane-content">
          <p>claude</p>
          <p class="muted">/plan-features</p>
          <p class="muted">/idea</p>
          <p class="muted">/status</p>
          <p class="muted">/check</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="layout-right">
        <div class="layout-pane layout-development">
          <div class="layout-pane-title">Development</div>
          <div class="layout-pane-content">
            <p>claude</p>
            <p class="muted">(active feature)</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="layout-pane layout-terminal">
          <div class="layout-pane-title">Terminal</div>
          <div class="layout-pane-content">
            <p>raw shell</p>
            <p class="muted">npm run dev</p>
            <p class="muted">git, tests</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Left pane (large)</strong> &mdash; Planning. Runs Claude Code for <code>/plan-features</code>, <code>/idea</code>, <code>/status</code>, <code>/check</code>, and other utility commands. This is your primary focus pane.</li>
      <li><strong>Right top pane</strong> &mdash; Development. Runs Claude Code for the active feature (<code>/new-feature</code>, phase work, <code>/wrap</code>).</li>
      <li><strong>Right bottom pane</strong> &mdash; Terminal. A raw shell for dev servers (<code>npm run dev</code>), <code>git</code> commands, file browsing, or manual tests. No Claude Code here.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Automatic setup</h3>
    <p>When you run <code>/setup</code> inside Warp, VibeCrew <strong>automatically generates a launch configuration</strong> for your project. This works for both new projects (<code>/new-project</code>) and existing ones (<code>/onboard</code>) since both start with <code>/setup</code>.</p>

    <p>During setup, VibeCrew asks which Claude Code command to use for this project. This is useful when you have multiple Claude Code profiles &mdash; for example, a personal config and a work config:</p>

    <TableWrap>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Command</th><th>Use Case</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>claude</code></td><td>Default &mdash; no profile flag</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>claude-p</code></td><td>Personal/private profile</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>claude-w</code></td><td>Work profile</td></tr>
          <tr><td><em>custom</em></td><td>Any alias or wrapper script you use</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </TableWrap>

    <p>The chosen command is stored in <code>.vibecrew/config.json</code> as <code>claude_command</code> and used in the generated launch config. You can change it later from the <strong>Vibe Dashboard</strong> (Settings &rarr; Terminal &rarr; Claude Command) or by editing the config file directly.</p>

    <p>The generated launch config file is saved to:</p>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">file location</span>~/.warp/launch_configurations/&lt;project-name&gt;.yaml</code></pre>

    <p>For example, a project in <code>~/projects/my-app</code> creates <code>~/.warp/launch_configurations/my-app.yaml</code>. The project name is derived from the directory name.</p>

    <p>To launch it, open Warp's Command Palette (<kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>), type "Launch Configuration", and select your project by name. The three-pane layout opens with Claude Code already running in the Planning and Development panes.</p>

    <Callout type="info" title="Detection &amp; idempotency">
      <p>VibeCrew only generates the launch config when it detects Warp (via the <code>$WARP_SESSION_ID</code> environment variable). If a config already exists for the project, it is preserved &mdash; your customizations are never overwritten. Running <code>/setup</code> again is always safe.</p>
    </Callout>

    <h3>Customizing your layout</h3>
    <p>The generated config is a standard Warp YAML file that you can edit freely. All launch configs live in a single directory:</p>
    <pre class="copyable" data-copy="open ~/.warp/launch_configurations/"><button class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy to clipboard"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="9" y="9" width="13" height="13" rx="2"/><path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"/></svg></button><code><span class="code-label">bash</span>open ~/.warp/launch_configurations/</code></pre>

    <p>Here's the default structure that VibeCrew generates. The <code>exec</code> value matches whatever Claude command you chose during setup:</p>

    <pre class="copyable" data-copy="---
name: my-app
windows:
  - tabs:
      - title: my-app
        color: blue
        layout:
          split_direction: horizontal
          panes:
            - cwd: /Users/you/projects/my-app
              commands:
                - exec: claude-w
              is_focused: true
            - split_direction: vertical
              panes:
                - cwd: /Users/you/projects/my-app
                  commands:
                    - exec: claude-w
                - cwd: /Users/you/projects/my-app"><button class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy to clipboard"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="9" y="9" width="13" height="13" rx="2"/><path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"/></svg></button><code><span class="code-label">~/.warp/launch_configurations/my-app.yaml</span>---
name: my-app
windows:
  - tabs:
      - title: my-app
        color: blue
        layout:
          split_direction: horizontal
          panes:
            - cwd: /Users/you/projects/my-app
              commands:
                - exec: claude-w
              is_focused: true
            - split_direction: vertical
              panes:
                - cwd: /Users/you/projects/my-app
                  commands:
                    - exec: claude-w
                - cwd: /Users/you/projects/my-app</code></pre>

    <p>Common customizations:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Change the Claude command</strong> &mdash; replace the <code>exec:</code> value (e.g. <code>claude</code>, <code>claude-p</code>, <code>claude-w</code>) to switch profiles</li>
      <li><strong>Change the tab color</strong> &mdash; set <code>color</code> to <code>red</code>, <code>green</code>, <code>yellow</code>, <code>blue</code>, <code>magenta</code>, or <code>cyan</code> (must be lowercase)</li>
      <li><strong>Add a startup command</strong> &mdash; add <code>- exec: npm run dev</code> to the bottom-right pane's <code>commands</code> list to auto-start your dev server</li>
      <li><strong>Remove a Claude pane</strong> &mdash; delete the <code>commands</code> block from any pane to keep it as a raw terminal</li>
      <li><strong>Change the split direction</strong> &mdash; swap <code>vertical</code> and <code>horizontal</code> to rearrange pane positions</li>
    </ul>

    <Callout type="tip" title="Changing the Claude command later">
      <p>If you need to switch profiles after setup, you have three options: edit the <code>exec:</code> lines in the YAML file directly, update <code>claude_command</code> in <code>.vibecrew/config.json</code>, or change it in the Vibe Dashboard under Settings &rarr; Terminal. Note that changing the config only affects future launch config generation &mdash; to update an existing launch config, edit the YAML file.</p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout type="tip" title="Color-code your projects">
      <p>Assign a unique color to each project so you can identify them at a glance when multiple tabs are open. When a notification fires, the tab color tells you which project needs attention.</p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout type="info" title="Pane navigation">
      <p>Use <kbd>Opt</kbd>+<kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>Arrow</kbd> to move between panes by direction. Press <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> to temporarily maximize a pane for focused reading, then press it again to restore the layout.</p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout type="tip" title="Open configs as tabs, not windows">
      <p>By default, launching a configuration opens a new window. To open it as a <strong>tab in your current window</strong> instead, use <kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> when selecting the config from the Command Palette or the <strong>+</strong> menu. This keeps all your projects in one window for easier tab switching.</p>
    </Callout>

    <h3>Saving your current layout</h3>
    <p>Rearranged your panes and want to save the result? Open the Command Palette (<kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>), search for "Save New Launch Configuration", give it a name, and Warp captures your current tabs, panes, and directories. This overwrites the auto-generated YAML with your actual layout.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ KEYBOARD ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="keyboard">
    <span class="section-label">Speed</span>
    <h2>Essential Keyboard Shortcuts</h2>
    <p>These are the shortcuts that matter most when running multiple VibeCrew sessions. macOS shortcuts shown &mdash; on Windows/Linux, replace <kbd>CMD</kbd> with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>.</p>

    <h3>Tab navigation</h3>
    <TableWrap>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Action</th><th>Shortcut</th><th>When to Use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Jump to tab 1&ndash;8</strong></td><td><kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> through <kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>8</kbd></td><td>Fastest way to switch between projects. Tab 1 = Project A, Tab 2 = Project B, etc.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Jump to last tab</strong></td><td><kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>9</kbd></td><td>Quick access to your last project tab regardless of tab count</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Next / previous tab</strong></td><td><kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>&#125;</kbd> / <kbd>&#123;</kbd></td><td>Cycle through tabs when you're not sure which number</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Reopen closed tab</strong></td><td><kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd></td><td>Accidentally closed a session? Reopen within 60 seconds</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </TableWrap>

    <h3>Pane navigation</h3>
    <TableWrap>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Action</th><th>Shortcut</th><th>When to Use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Split right</strong></td><td><kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd></td><td>Add a side-by-side pane for quick tasks without leaving your tab</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Split down</strong></td><td><kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd></td><td>Add a pane below for log tailing or test watching</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Navigate panes</strong></td><td><kbd>Opt</kbd>+<kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>Arrow</kbd></td><td>Move between panes by direction</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Maximize / restore pane</strong></td><td><kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd></td><td>Temporarily full-screen a pane to read long output, then restore</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </TableWrap>

    <h3>Block navigation</h3>
    <TableWrap>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Action</th><th>Shortcut</th><th>When to Use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Previous / next block</strong></td><td><kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>Up</kbd> / <kbd>Down</kbd></td><td>Jump between commands. Crucial for reviewing what Claude Code did while you were away</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Bookmark block</strong></td><td><kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>B</kbd></td><td>Mark important output (e.g. test results, Vibe Score) for quick return</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Jump between bookmarks</strong></td><td><kbd>Opt</kbd>+<kbd>Up</kbd> / <kbd>Down</kbd></td><td>Navigate bookmarked blocks without scrolling through everything</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Search in block</strong></td><td><kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd></td><td>Find specific text in a long output block</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Filter block output</strong></td><td><kbd>Opt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd></td><td>Reduce output to only matching lines &mdash; great for filtering test results or error logs</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Clear all blocks</strong></td><td><kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd></td><td>Clean slate before a new task</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </TableWrap>

    <h3>General</h3>
    <TableWrap>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Action</th><th>Shortcut</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Command Palette</strong></td><td><kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Settings</strong></td><td><kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>,</kbd></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </TableWrap>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ BLOCKS ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="blocks">
    <span class="section-label">Output Management</span>
    <h2>Working with Blocks</h2>
    <p>Warp's block model is exceptionally useful for AI-assisted development. Every command and its output are grouped into a discrete block that you can navigate, search, copy, filter, and share independently.</p>

    <h3>Why blocks matter for VibeCrew</h3>
    <p>When Claude Code implements a feature, it produces dozens of commands with hundreds of lines of output. In a traditional terminal, this is one endless scroll. In Warp, each command is a navigable block.</p>

    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Sticky command headers</h4>
        <p>Scrolling through long output? The command that produced it stays pinned at the top. You always know which operation you're looking at.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Failed command highlighting</h4>
        <p>Commands that exit non-zero get a red sidebar. When you return to a tab after a notification, failed blocks are immediately visible &mdash; no scanning required.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Block sharing</h4>
        <p>Right-click any block &rarr; Share. Generates a web permalink with formatting preserved. Perfect for pasting test failures or Vibe Score results into Slack or PRs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Block filtering</h4>
        <p><kbd>Opt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> on a block reduces output to matching lines only. Use it to filter test output to just failures, or grep CI logs for the error line.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Block workflow for reviewing agent output</h3>
    <p>When you switch to a tab where Claude Code has been working:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>Up</kbd> repeatedly to jump through the blocks it produced</li>
      <li>Red sidebar? That block failed &mdash; investigate</li>
      <li><kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>B</kbd> to bookmark anything you want to revisit (test results, the <code>/wrap</code> Vibe Score)</li>
      <li>Later, <kbd>Opt</kbd>+<kbd>Up</kbd>/<kbd>Down</kbd> to jump directly between your bookmarks</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ WARP DRIVE ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="warp-drive">
    <span class="section-label">Team Collaboration</span>
    <h2>Warp Drive for Teams</h2>
    <p>Warp Drive is a cloud-synced knowledge base built into the terminal. It stores four types of objects: <strong>Workflows</strong>, <strong>Notebooks</strong>, <strong>Prompts</strong>, and <strong>Environment Variables</strong>. For VibeCrew teams, it's the best way to standardize tooling.</p>

    <h3>Shared VibeCrew workflows</h3>
    <p>Warp Workflows are parameterized command templates. Create one for common VibeCrew operations and share it with your team via a Team Workspace.</p>

    <h4>Example: Start a feature</h4>
    <p>Create this as a Warp Workflow (via Command Palette &rarr; "Create a New Personal Workflow"):</p>

    <pre><code><span class="code-label">warp workflow</span>Name:    VibeCrew: Start Feature
Command: cd &#123;&#123;project_path&#125;&#125; &amp;&amp; claude-p '/new-feature "&#123;&#123;feature_name&#125;&#125;"'</code></pre>

    <p>Arguments use <code>&#123;&#123;double_curly_braces&#125;&#125;</code> syntax. When you run the workflow via <kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>, Warp prompts you to fill in each argument. Press <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd> to cycle between them.</p>

    <h4>Example workflows worth creating</h4>
    <TableWrap>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Workflow Name</th><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>VibeCrew: Start Feature</strong></td>
            <td><code>cd &#123;&#123;project&#125;&#125; &amp;&amp; claude-p '/new-feature "&#123;&#123;name&#125;&#125;"'</code></td>
            <td>Start a feature session with one command</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>VibeCrew: Quick Status</strong></td>
            <td><code>cd &#123;&#123;project&#125;&#125; &amp;&amp; claude-p '/status'</code></td>
            <td>Check project state without entering interactive mode</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>VibeCrew: Heal CI</strong></td>
            <td><code>cd &#123;&#123;project&#125;&#125; &amp;&amp; claude-p '/heal'</code></td>
            <td>Fix CI failures in one shot</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>VibeCrew: Run Backlog</strong></td>
            <td><code>cd &#123;&#123;project&#125;&#125; &amp;&amp; claude-p '/run-backlog'</code></td>
            <td>Fire-and-forget batch execution</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </TableWrap>

    <h4>Dynamic enum arguments</h4>
    <p>Warp Workflows support <strong>dynamic enums</strong> &mdash; argument values generated by a shell command at runtime. Use this for a feature-name argument that pulls from your actual backlog:</p>

    <pre><code><span class="code-label">dynamic enum</span>Argument: feature_name
Type:     Dynamic Enum
Command:  jq -r '.features[].id' .vibecrew/backlog.json</code></pre>

    <p>Now when you run the workflow, Warp shows a dropdown of your actual backlog features instead of a free-text field. No typos, no guessing.</p>

    <h4>Sharing with your team</h4>
    <p>Move a workflow from your Personal Workspace to the Team Workspace in Warp Drive. It syncs in real-time to all team members. Everyone gets the same standardized VibeCrew commands.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ NOTEBOOKS ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="notebooks">
    <span class="section-label">Documentation</span>
    <h2>Notebooks as Runbooks</h2>
    <p>Warp Notebooks combine Markdown text with executable command blocks. They're the perfect format for VibeCrew onboarding guides and operational runbooks that your team can execute step-by-step, directly in the terminal.</p>

    <h3>Create a team onboarding notebook</h3>
    <p>Open Warp Drive, click <strong>+</strong> &rarr; <strong>New Notebook</strong>. Mix documentation with executable commands:</p>

    <pre><code><span class="code-label">notebook example</span>### VibeCrew Setup for New Team Members

Welcome! Follow these steps to set up VibeCrew on your machine.

**Step 1: Install dependencies**
&gt; brew install terminal-notifier jq gh

**Step 2: Clone the project**
&gt; git clone &#123;&#123;repo_url&#125;&#125;
&gt; cd &#123;&#123;project_name&#125;&#125;

**Step 3: Run VibeCrew setup**
&gt; claude
Then type:
&gt; /setup

**Step 4: Verify notifications**
The setup wizard sends a test notification. Click it to confirm deep-linking works.</code></pre>

    <p>Command blocks (the <code>&gt;</code> lines) have an insert button &mdash; click it or press <kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> to execute the command in your active terminal session. Parameters use the same <code>&#123;&#123;curly_brace&#125;&#125;</code> syntax as Workflows.</p>

    <h3>Useful notebooks to create</h3>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h4>New developer onboarding</h4>
        <p>Dependencies, plugin install, Warp config, first <code>/setup</code> run, notification test. Executable from top to bottom.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Release checklist</h4>
        <p><code>/status</code> check, <code>/audit</code> security scan, test run, changelog review, git tag, deploy command. Each step executable in-place.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Incident response</h4>
        <p>Check CI status, pull logs, run <code>/heal</code>, verify fix, notify team. A runbook that's executable under pressure.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>Notebooks sync across the team via Warp Drive and export as Markdown (<code>.md</code>) for version control.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ ENV VARS ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="env-vars">
    <span class="section-label">Configuration</span>
    <h2>Environment Variables</h2>
    <p>Warp Drive can manage environment variables and inject them into terminal sessions on demand. This is useful for switching between project environments or sharing non-secret configuration across the team.</p>

    <h3>Per-project variable sets</h3>
    <p>Create a Warp Drive variable set for each project or environment:</p>

    <pre><code><span class="code-label">warp drive variable set: "TripFlow Dev"</span>PROJECT_ROOT=/Users/you/projects/tripflow
SUPABASE_URL=https://abc123.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000</code></pre>

    <p>Load it into your session from Warp Drive or the Command Palette. Variables persist for the session duration.</p>

    <h3>Secret management</h3>
    <p>Warp supports <strong>dynamic variables</strong> that retrieve secrets from external managers at runtime &mdash; Warp stores the retrieval command, never the actual secret.</p>

    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h4>1Password CLI</h4>
        <p>Warp integrates with <code>op</code> CLI. Select secrets from your vault without leaving the terminal. The secret is injected for the session only.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Custom commands</h4>
        <p>Point to any secret manager: AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault, or a custom script. Warp executes the command and injects the result.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Callout type="warn" title="Don't store secrets as static variables">
      <p>Warp Drive static variables are <strong>not</strong> designed for sensitive data. Use dynamic variables with 1Password or your team's secret manager for API keys, database credentials, and tokens.</p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ WARP AI ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="warp-ai">
    <span class="section-label">AI Integration</span>
    <h2>Warp AI + Claude Code</h2>
    <p>Warp has its own AI features (Agent Mode, Warp Pair, Warp Dispatch). These are <strong>complementary</strong> to Claude Code, not conflicting. They're separate systems with different strengths.</p>

    <TableWrap>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Capability</th><th>Warp AI</th><th>Claude Code + VibeCrew</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Quick command help</strong></td>
            <td>Warp Agent Mode explains errors, suggests fixes for failed commands</td>
            <td>Not its purpose &mdash; VibeCrew is for full feature development</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Feature development</strong></td>
            <td>Warp Dispatch can run autonomously but lacks VibeCrew's architecture enforcement</td>
            <td>Full lifecycle: foundation, TDR, 6-phase cycle, Vibe Score, self-improving rules</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Multi-model support</strong></td>
            <td>Claude, GPT, Gemini</td>
            <td>Claude only (Opus, Sonnet, Haiku selected per agent role)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Context sharing</strong></td>
            <td>Separate context from Claude Code</td>
            <td>Persistent via CLAUDE.md, handoffs, session logs</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </TableWrap>

    <h3>When to use which</h3>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Use Warp AI for</h4>
        <p>Quick one-off questions: "What does this error mean?", "How do I undo the last 3 git commits?", "Convert this curl to fetch". Terminal-native problems that don't need project context.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Use Claude Code + VibeCrew for</h4>
        <p>Everything involving your project: feature development, architecture decisions, code reviews, testing, CI healing. VibeCrew provides the structure, scoring, and learning system that Warp AI doesn't have.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Callout type="info" title="They don't conflict">
      <p>Warp AI and Claude Code run in separate contexts and don't interfere with each other. You can ask Warp's agent a quick question in the same tab where Claude Code is running. The two never see each other's context.</p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

