  <!-- ============ SESSION RESTORE ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="session-restore">
    <span class="section-label">Reliability</span>
    <h2>Session Restoration</h2>
    <p>Warp saves your window layout, tabs, panes, and recent blocks to a local database on every quit. When you reopen Warp, everything is restored exactly as you left it.</p>

    <h3>What gets restored</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Window positions and sizes</strong></li>
      <li><strong>All tabs</strong> with their titles and colors</li>
      <li><strong>Pane layouts</strong> within each tab</li>
      <li><strong>Working directories</strong> for each pane</li>
      <li><strong>Recent blocks</strong> (command history and output)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What doesn't get restored</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Running processes.</strong> Claude Code sessions end when Warp quits. You'll need to restart them. This is why <code>/wrap</code> before closing is critical &mdash; it saves your progress to <code>.vibecrew/</code> state files that survive restarts</li>
      <li><strong>Bookmarks.</strong> Block bookmarks are per-session only</li>
    </ul>

    <Callout type="tip" title="The restart workflow">
      <p>Always <code>/wrap</code> before quitting Warp. When you reopen, your tabs and directories are restored. Start Claude Code in each tab (<code>claude</code>) and the Session Startup agent reads the latest handoff document &mdash; it knows exactly where you left off. The combination of Warp session restore + VibeCrew handoffs means you lose zero context across restarts.</p>
    </Callout>

    <h3>Configuration</h3>
    <p>Session restoration is enabled by default. To toggle: Warp Settings &rarr; Features &rarr; "Restore windows, tabs, and panes on startup."</p>

    <p>Closed a tab by accident? <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd> reopens the most recently closed tab within 60 seconds.</p>
  </section>

